THE SHAPE (neutral reproducer, run on the tree, perl 5.40.3 vs PCL):

    my @a = (1, 2, 3, 4);
    @a[0, 3, 1, 2] = @a;            print "@a\n";        # perl 1 3 4 2   PCL 1 3 2 2
    my @b = (1, 2, 3);
    @b[1, 2] = @b[0, 1];            print "@b\n";        # both 1 1 2     (a slice right side is already a copy)
    my %h = (x => 1, y => 2);
    @h{qw(x y)} = @h{qw(y x)};      print "$h{x} $h{y}\n";   # both 2 1
    my @c = (1, 2, 3);
    @c[2, 1, 0] = @c;               print "@c\n";        # perl 3 2 1     PCL 1 2 1

WHERE (emitted Lisp): `(p-setf (p-aslice @a 0 3 1 2) @a)` -- the right side is the live array vector, and the slice store walks it while writing into it.  The whole-array `@x = ...` assignment and the list form `($a, $b) = ($b, $a)` already copy (pp_aassign's rule); the slice-target arm of p-setf does not.  The fix is a copy of the right side when it is (or may alias) the target's container -- the s440-era `p-list-=` COMMON test (#910) is the precedent for doing it only when needed.

CHEAP DISCRIMINATING MEASUREMENT (taken): writing the right side as a fresh list, `@{[ @a ]}`, makes 1198 identical to perl -- its only cause; and the slice-on-the-right rows are right, so it is the bare-array right side only.

THE PROGRAM: 1198-Round-robin-tournament-schedule rotates the team list with one slice assignment from the array itself (the circle method); every round after the first has a team paired with itself.


